WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.709
Film Courage: You had referenced the movie
THANK YOU FOR SMOKING in at least of of your

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:05.020
videos [on Adam’s Youtube channel].

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.490
Forgive me, I haven’t seen the movie but
I want to make a point of seeing and I’ve

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.230
heard from so many people that it’s amazing.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.860
Adam Skelter, author/story artist: It’s
SO good!

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:11.860
It’s amazing.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.480
Aaron Eckhart is so charismatic in it.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.619
Film Courage: And where he [the character
Nick Naylor] says he has this BA in…

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:19.769
Adam: …Public Relations…Oh?

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:21.300
A BA in bull?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.460
Film Courage: Right!

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.119
And he’s the kind of guy that can talk a
girl into anything.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:31.430
And so in that movie [THANK YOU FOR SMOKING]
how would you break down the text, the subtext

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:32.470
and the context?

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.310
How much do you think [the theme] this relates
to the entertainment business in the sense

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:40.200
that they are trying to make it seem that
smoking is okay, they are trying to portray

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.940
that all of this is okay.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.980
Adam: See what I think is interesting, I mean
ultimately I love that movie because it’s

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:51.070
a polemic, it’s presented as morally, it’s
about moral ambiguity.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:01:00.670
There’s this larger theme which is basically
saying like culture…this is a really complex

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.500
breakdown, it’s a complex movie I love it,
it’s one of my favorites.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:11.220
But at its core it’s about this guy who
is trying to sell smoking but what he’s

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.580
really trying to do…I mean it’s an exercise
in rhetoric but what he’s really trying

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.780
to do is say I don’t want people to determine
my life for me.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.780
I want to take control of my own life.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.700
I get to decide if I want throat cancer or
not.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.320
Which is why at the very end you’re like
if my kid wants to smoke I’m going to light

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.530
it up for him.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.150
Because it’s not really about him saying
like it’s okay to smoke.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:43.990
The whole story is this journey of him facing
these…largely it’s one of the themes that’s

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:50.560
really close to me which is large tribal groups
trying to impose their values on individuals

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.120
and his individuality coming back and saying
“No, I’m going to do what I want to do

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:00.560
and you’re kind of a villain for trying
to impose that on other people.”

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.700
And it does it in such a clever, polemic way
and smoking is really just the pretext that

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.980
gets us into it.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.580
It’s something that most people largely
condemn.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:16.560
I don’t smoke and it’s not healthy but
at the same time who are you to decide that

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.819
for other people and that’s what that movie
is asking about but from a GREAT rhetorical

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:24.000
position.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:33.761
Film Courage: So would you say there are similar
themes [in the movie] in terms of story, the

00:02:33.761 --> 00:02:40.360
entertainment industry and what we view as
acceptable to watch?

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.270
I think Rob Lowe plays the studio executive
(I just watched a few clips) and he’s so

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.270
great.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.270
Adam: He’s so GOOD!

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.290
Film Courage: And he’s pitching people “Yeah
I think it’s like…”

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:52.870
And he names all of these blockbusters and
that’s how he sees the world is based on

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.070
all of these hit movies.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.470
Adam: See I don’t know?

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:02.650
I’ve been thinking about, especially the
entertainment industry and like a lot of people

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.709
especially on your channel, the interviews
you guys are doing lots of discussion about

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:12.550
you know what are the best rules for survival
in the entertainment industry.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:17.860
And for me there’s this metaphor that just
kind of describes is simply which is that

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:24.950
the entertainment industry is like a harbor
and we have a constantly shifting tide, the

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.230
water is going up and down.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.110
And that tide is the amount of tension that
people are willing to pay to things.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:36.810
Now the industry does the best they can…and
we’re talking by industry specifically we’re

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:43.790
talking about studios, distributors, production
companies they are trying to understand which

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:53.849
way the water is going, where it’s moving
and most of the time as a writer you are trying

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:59.879
to swim in these choppy seas because you’ve
built your craft and you’re trying to dock

00:03:59.879 --> 00:04:00.879
in the harbor.

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:04.800
Now the studios have these docks and they’re
the harbor, right?

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.840
And because the water is constantly shifting
the best you can do is make your craft as

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.269
adaptable to whatever the needs of the harbor
are.

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:22.460
So they are looking for whatever is going
to draw in…this metaphor may break down

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.129
a bit if we get too specific.

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:32.110
But the idea is basically everybody has to
adapt to the shifting tide and the studios

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.650
especially they have the most at stake.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:42.310
So they are looking for stories or writers,
craftspersons that have built crafts that

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:48.789
are adaptable to the shifting tides and you
know there are some studios that have built

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:54.240
these massive mega harbors and your little
dinghy comes up and it’s just going to get

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.410
beat up against the water because it will
be swallowed up because the water is changing

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.410
so much.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:05.639
Now especially with the changing of streaming
and distribution models are radically shifting

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:12.789
and indie film is shifting and people are
making movies for less than a million dollars

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:18.779
the harbor is dramatically different than
it was five years ago, ten years ago.

00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:24.119
So when it comes to the industry, all you’re
trying to do is find your path in.

00:05:24.119 --> 00:05:29.319
The best thing you can do is focus on developing
your craft as much as possible.

00:05:29.319 --> 00:05:35.229
Making it as adaptable as possible which is
really just the process of being able to delve

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:40.580
into your metaphors and really work on being
able to tell the stories that are compelling

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:45.400
to other people and no one has the right answer
to that.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.889
We’re all just figuring that out.

